# apps/api/.env.example
#
# Reference for every environment variable the production API server
# (apps/api/cmd/api) reads. Copy this file to a real `.env` on the
# staging host (untracked - never commit) and fill in real values.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for which env vars the
# server expects: LoadProductionConfig in apps/api/app/api/production.go
# is the matching reader, and VOC-032-TEST-05 diffs the env names
# declared here against the names that reader actually consumes.
#
# Conventions:
#   * No real credential is committed. Every value below is a
#     placeholder, an obvious fake, or an explicit "unset" sentinel.
#   * Required vars (no usable default) are marked [REQUIRED]. The
#     server refuses to start if any required var is missing.
#   * Vars with a default in code are marked [default: ...]. The
#     default applies if the var is absent; setting the var
#     overrides it.
#   * Vars driven by a DOC-11 kill switch are marked [kill switch].
#     Set to "false" to disable the feature; the server keeps
#     running and returns a stable, documented response instead of
#     crashing.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core server
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [default: 8080] TCP port the HTTP server binds to. The container
# publishes this port internally only; nginx (apps/web nginx in
# docker-compose) is the only service exposed to the host.
PORT=8080

# [REQUIRED] PostgreSQL connection string the server opens at
# startup. The server pings the database before serving traffic and
# fails fast if the ping fails - a half-configured server never
# starts. Format: postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DBNAME?sslmode=...
DATABASE_URL=postgres://vocanova:change-me@postgres:5432/vocanova?sslmode=disable

# [default: staging] Free-form environment tag (e.g. "staging",
# "production"). Recorded in the auth service and aifeedback's
# ServiceConfig.Release field; never interpreted as a code path.
ENVIRONMENT=staging

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth and session
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [REQUIRED] Public base URL of THIS API, used to build absolute
# magic-link URLs and OAuth callback URLs the auth service emails
# out. For staging, this is https://api-staging.vocanova.site
# (nginx front of the api container - see docker-compose).
BASE_URL=https://api-staging.vocanova.site

# [default: /auth/magic] Path component the magic-link email points
# at, joined to BASE_URL. The web app handles the corresponding
# client-side route.
MAGIC_LINK_PATH=/auth/magic

# [REQUIRED] OAuth redirect URI registered with the upstream
# identity provider (Google, etc.). Must match exactly what the
# provider's OAuth client is configured to allow, including
# scheme/host/path. The T15 real Google OAuth provider uses this
# same value as its GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI (see apps/web
# .env.example for the founder-side comment about the redirect URI
# being a single shared value across web and api).
OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://api-staging.vocanova.site/auth/oauth/google/callback

# [optional] Comma-separated list of additional OAuth return-to
# URLs the auth service will accept as post-callback redirects
# back to the web app. If unset, the auth service accepts exactly
# the same URI as OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI (no web-app bounce). Set this
# to e.g. https://staging.vocanova.site/onboarding,https://staging.vocanova.site/home
# when the web app needs to round-trip the user through onboarding
# or home after a successful sign-in. Comment out the line below
# to skip setting the var.
#
# This list ALSO derives the CORS allowed-origins allowlist
# (app/api/cors.go's corsAllowedOrigins) - the scheme+host+port of
# each URL here (path stripped) is exactly what's allowed to make
# credentialed cross-origin requests against this API. There is no
# separate CORS-specific env var: since the API and the web app are
# deployed on sibling subdomains (VOC-037-D00), any web origin
# trusted enough to receive an OAuth callback redirect is exactly
# the trust boundary CORS needs too, and keeping one list avoids
# the two silently drifting apart.
OAUTH_REDIRECT_ALLOWLIST=https://staging.vocanova.site/onboarding,https://staging.vocanova.site/home

# [REQUIRED] Domain attribute on the session cookie. Must match the
# domain the browser sees the cookie under; for staging this is
# .vocanova.site (leading dot lets it cover both the apex and the
# api-staging subdomain) or a narrower value if cookies are scoped
# to one host only.
SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.vocanova.site

# [default: true] Whether the session cookie is marked Secure
# (HTTPS-only). Keep true in any environment served over TLS,
# including staging; set to "false" only for local plaintext
# development against `pnpm dev` style mocks.
SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true

# [default: 720h] Lifetime of a session cookie, parsed as a Go
# time.Duration string (e.g. "720h", "30m", "24h"). 720h = 30 days,
# matching the auth service's documented session window.
SESSION_LIFETIME=720h

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI feedback provider (aifeedback)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The AI_PROVIDER_* vars below configure the selected AI provider used for
# BOTH sentence-feedback generation and content moderation. AI_PROVIDER
# accepts "opencode" (default), "gemini", and "cloudflare". The wiring lives in
# apps/api/app/api/production.go's buildAIProviders helper.
#
# The values assigned below are the OpenCode-shaped defaults, because
# AI_PROVIDER's own default is "opencode". To switch a host to Gemini,
# three edits are required in this block - see the per-var comments:
#   1. set AI_PROVIDER=gemini
#   2. set AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY to the Gemini API key
#   3. for Gemini, comment out (or clear) AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL and
#      AI_PROVIDER_MODEL so Gemini's own endpoint/model defaults apply
#   4. for Cloudflare, set AI_PROVIDER_ACCOUNT_ID
# Leaving step 3 undone sends Gemini requests to the OpenCode host with an
# OpenCode model identifier. For Cloudflare, an empty account ID means the
# production wiring falls back to the mock provider instead of constructing
# a partial Cloudflare client.

# [default: opencode] AI provider identifier.
#   - "opencode" selects aifeedback.NewOpenCodeFeedbackProvider and
#     aifeedback.NewOpenCodeModerationProvider
#   - "gemini" selects aifeedback.NewGeminiFeedbackProvider and
#     aifeedback.NewGeminiModerationProvider
#   - "cloudflare" selects aifeedback.NewCloudflareFeedbackProvider and
#     aifeedback.NewCloudflareModerationProvider (requires both
#     AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY and AI_PROVIDER_ACCOUNT_ID)
# Any other value falls back to the mock provider used by tests.
AI_PROVIDER=opencode

# Base URL of the selected provider's HTTP endpoint.
# [default: http://127.0.0.1:4096] when AI_PROVIDER=opencode - OpenCode
#   is operator-hosted, so it needs this to point at an
#   `opencode serve` host.
# [no default, and not required] when AI_PROVIDER=gemini - Gemini's REST
#   endpoint (https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com) is a fixed
#   Google host applied inside aifeedback.GeminiConfig when this var is
#   unset. Setting it overrides Gemini's endpoint too (useful only for a
#   test harness or a Gemini-compatible proxy), so comment the line
#   below out when running Gemini unless you mean to override it.
# [no default, and not required] when AI_PROVIDER=cloudflare - Cloudflare's
#   REST host (https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4) is applied inside
#   aifeedback.CloudflareConfig when this var is unset. Set this only when
#   targeting a test harness or a proxy endpoint.
AI_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4096

# [optional] API key for the selected provider. OpenCode sends
# `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Gemini sends `x-goog-api-key: <key>`
# (a single API key, no OAuth; created at aistudio.google.com for the
# free tier). Cloudflare also uses this var as its bearer token. Required
# for a real provider; if unset, production wiring falls back to the mock
# provider for both paths. Never commit a real key; load it from the
# staging host's untracked .env only.
# Comment out the line below to skip setting the var.
AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY=change-me-real-opencode-key

# [REQUIRED when AI_PROVIDER=cloudflare, unused otherwise] Cloudflare account
# identifier used as the URL path segment in Workers AI calls:
# /accounts/{account_id}/ai/run/{model}. This value is not a secret on its
# own, but Cloudflare provider wiring requires it together with
# AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY; when either is missing, production wiring falls back
# to the mock provider instead of constructing a partial Cloudflare client.
# Comment out the line below to skip setting the var.
AI_PROVIDER_ACCOUNT_ID=change-me-cloudflare-account-id

# Model identifier for the selected provider (read for both feedback
# generation and content moderation). The two providers use different
# model-identifier namespaces, so this value is provider-specific:
# [default: opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro] when AI_PROVIDER=opencode
# [default: gemini-2.5-flash] when AI_PROVIDER=gemini
# [default: @cf/meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct-fp8-fast] when
# AI_PROVIDER=cloudflare
# Each default applies only when this var is unset, so comment the line
# below out (or set an explicit Gemini model such as gemini-2.5-flash)
# when running Gemini, or set a Cloudflare model if you are overriding
# Cloudflare's default.
AI_PROVIDER_MODEL=opencode-go/deepseek-v4-pro

# [default: 8s] Per-request timeout against the selected provider,
# parsed as a Go time.Duration string. Applies to BOTH the feedback
# call and the moderation call; each call is a separate HTTP request
# with its own client timeout, so the combined worst-case
# moderation+feedback latency is roughly 2x this value. Increase
# only with care: long timeouts extend the request window the API
# holds open against sentence-practice and review writes.
# Reconciling the combined latency against DOC-09 §18's 10s total
# backend target is deferred to VOC-032-T10's live threshold
# evaluation (a separate task).
AI_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT=8s

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOC-11 kill switches (per docs/operations/11-devops-and-ci-cd.md §3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [default: true] [kill switch] Master switch for AI feedback
# generation. When "false", aifeedback.NewDisabledGate is wired in
# and every AI-feedback route returns a stable, documented
# non-crash response; non-AI routes are unaffected.
AI_FEATURES_ENABLED=true

# [default: true] [kill switch] Magic-link email sign-in. When
# "false", the auth service rejects magic-link requests. The real
# email sender (T14) and the Fake{} sender are both routed through
# this switch. The T14 real HTTPSender is wired in only when this
# switch is "true" AND the email-provider credential (see below) is
# set; when either condition fails the wiring falls back to Fake{}
# so staging can still run with magic-link delivery off rather
# than crashing at startup.
EMAIL_MAGIC_LINK_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transactional email provider (T14 real email.Sender)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The T14 real email.Sender (apps/api/foundation/email.HTTPSender)
# is provider-agnostic: it POSTs JSON to EMAIL_PROVIDER_URL with an
# `Authorization: Bearer <EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY>` header and a
# `{"from","to","subject","text","html"}` body. This shape is
# compatible with Resend, SendGrid v3, and Postmark's token-auth
# mode out of the box; providers with a different wire format can
# be supported by a future, narrower T14-follow-up package.
#
# All four vars below are optional: when EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY is
# unset (or EMAIL_MAGIC_LINK_ENABLED is "false"), the production
# wiring falls back to email.Fake{} and no real email is sent.
# Required vars (URL, API key, From) are validated by NewHTTPSender
# at startup when the credential is set; a misconfigured URL or
# missing From is a hard startup error, not a silent fallback.

# [optional] HTTPS endpoint of the transactional-email provider
# (e.g. https://api.resend.com/emails for Resend,
# https://api.sendgrid.com/v3/mail/send for SendGrid v3,
# https://api.postmarkapp.com/email for Postmark token-auth). When
# unset while EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY is set, NewProductionAPI
# returns a startup error. Comment out the line below to skip
# setting the var.
EMAIL_PROVIDER_URL=https://api.resend.com/emails

# [optional] API key the HTTPSender sends as
# `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Required for the real sender; if
# unset the production wiring falls back to Fake{}. Never commit a
# real key; load it from the staging host's untracked .env only.
# Comment out the line below to skip setting the var.
EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY=change-me-real-provider-key

# [optional] `From` address the HTTPSender stamps on every outbound
# message. Format: "Display Name <[email protected]>" or just
# "[email protected]". Required when EMAIL_PROVIDER_API_KEY is set;
# must be a sender address the provider has been configured to
# allow for the API key in use. The staging mailbox this package
# uses is the one the founder provisions under VOC-032-DEP-07.
EMAIL_FROM=Vocanova <[email protected]>

# [default: 10s] Per-request timeout against the email provider,
# parsed as a Go time.Duration string. The HTTPSender uses a
# dedicated *http.Client with this timeout; increase only with
# care because the request blocks the auth service's
# RequestMagicLink path while the send is in flight.
EMAIL_PROVIDER_TIMEOUT=10s

# [default: true] [kill switch] Google OAuth sign-in. When
# "false", the auth service rejects Google OAuth initiation. The
# real Google OAuth provider (T15) and the FakeOAuthProvider are
# both routed through this switch.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google OAuth provider (T15 real auth.OAuthProvider)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The T15 real Google OAuth provider
# (apps/api/business/auth.GoogleOAuthProvider) implements
# auth.OAuthProvider against Google's real OAuth 2.0 endpoints
# (https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth for authorization,
# https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token for the code exchange,
# https://openidconnect.googleapis.com/v1/userinfo for the
# verified identity). Both GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID and
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET are required to wire the real
# provider; when either is unset (or GOOGLE_OAUTH_ENABLED is
# "false"), the production wiring falls back to
# auth.NewFakeOAuthProvider and no real Google sign-in is
# attempted. The same OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI value the auth service
# uses is reused as the Google OAuth client's redirect URI
# (the founder registers a single redirect URI per
# Google Cloud OAuth 2.0 client, matching the deployed API
# origin's /auth/oauth/google/callback path).
#
# All three vars below are optional: when GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID
# is unset (or GOOGLE_OAUTH_ENABLED is "false"), the production
# wiring falls back to FakeOAuthProvider and no real Google
# sign-in is attempted. A half-configured credential (client ID
# set but secret missing, or vice versa) is a hard startup error,
# not a silent fallback - silently falling back would hide a
# real configuration mistake.

# [optional] OAuth 2.0 client identifier Google issued for this
# app (Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials ->
# OAuth 2.0 Client IDs). Required for the real provider; if
# unset the production wiring falls back to FakeOAuthProvider.
# Never commit a real client ID before it has been revoked or
# rotated; the staging client ID is created by the founder under
# VOC-032-DEP-07. Comment out the line below to skip setting
# the var.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=change-me-real-google-client-id

# [optional] OAuth 2.0 client secret Google issued alongside the
# client ID. Required for the real provider; if unset the
# production wiring falls back to FakeOAuthProvider. Never
# commit a real secret; load it from the staging host's
# untracked .env only. The T15 provider never logs this value
# and never includes it in any returned error message. Comment
# out the line below to skip setting the var.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=change-me-real-google-client-secret

# [default: 8s] Per-request timeout against Google's token and
# userinfo endpoints, parsed as a Go time.Duration string. The
# GoogleOAuthProvider uses a dedicated *http.Client with this
# timeout for both calls; increase only with care because the
# request blocks the auth service's OAuthCallback path while
# the exchange is in flight.
GOOGLE_OAUTH_TIMEOUT=8s

# [default: openid email profile] Space-separated OAuth 2.0
# scopes the GoogleOAuthProvider requests at the authorization
# step. The default is the minimum scope set the auth service
# requires for verified-email sign-in (openid for the OIDC
# ID-token claim, email for the verified-email field, profile
# for the display name and avatar URL). Override only with a
# scope set the founder has confirmed Google will grant to the
# OAuth 2.0 client; a scope Google does not allow will surface
# as an "invalid_scope" error on the consent screen.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_SCOPES=openid email profile

# [default: true] [kill switch] New-user sign-up. When "false",
# the auth service refuses to create new accounts on any sign-in
# path; existing users can still sign in.
NEW_USER_SIGNUP_ENABLED=true

# [optional] [VOC-038-D00/D01] Comma-separated list of email
# addresses admitted to sign up even while NEW_USER_SIGNUP_ENABLED
# is "false" - the L1 controlled-launch allowlist. Has no effect
# when NEW_USER_SIGNUP_ENABLED is "true". Matching is
# case-insensitive and whitespace-trimmed. Empty/unset allowlists
# no one, matching pre-VOC-038 behavior. To expand the cohort, add
# an address here and redeploy - no code change required.
# NEW_USER_SIGNUP_ALLOWLIST=founder@vocanova.site

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Monitoring and alerting (VOC-037-T04)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [optional] Sentry DSN for backend error monitoring. When unset,
# the API starts with Sentry disabled and /ops/monitoring/sentry-test
# still requires MONITORING_TEST_TOKEN but emits no external event.
# Keep this value in the host secret file only; never commit it.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://examplePublicKey@o0.ingest.sentry.io/0

# [default: ENVIRONMENT value] Sentry environment tag attached to
# captured events. For production this should stay "production".
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production

# [optional] Release identifier attached to Sentry events. Set this
# to the deployed immutable image tag or commit SHA for easier
# rollback triage, e.g. "sha-abc1234".
# SENTRY_RELEASE=sha-abcdef0

# [optional but required to expose the route] Bearer token accepted by
# POST /ops/monitoring/sentry-test. Keep this token secret; it gates
# deliberate test-event emission so unauthenticated clients cannot
# spam monitoring.
# MONITORING_TEST_TOKEN=change-me-monitoring-token
